```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: red
REAL_USE_CASE: SGLang/vLLM-style inference servers running tool-using agents (e.g., Claude Code sessions, Codex) that cache file-prefix KV blocks with LRU radix/block eviction (SGLang RadixAttention 2312.07104, vLLM APC 2309.06180). Needed only when cache is tight (capacity fraction 0.1–0.3) and far-reuse share is ~0.19 (CC corpus far_share=0.188), where Belady saves up to 11.0% recompute mass over LRU.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only. The oracle gap is 11.0% at frac 0.1, 9.2% at 0.2, 6.4% at 0.3, collapsing to 3.1% at 0.5 and 0.6% at 0.7. The only cheap-policy point reaching >=0.50 captured (pin+LRU N=2 at 0.7) sits on a 0.6%-of-LRU gap. At the capacities where the gap matters (0.1–0.3), every cheap policy captures ≤0.31, with SGLang-LFU at -0.747 to -0.317 (worse than LRU). Production deployments run at higher residency where the absolute win is <3%; the tight-cache regime is a thrash-avoidance scenario, not a steady-state operating point.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [1] Negligible absolute win at realistic capacities (0.6–3.1% recompute); [2] SGLang-LFU is measurably worse than LRU at tight caps (-0.747 captured at 0.1), creating regression risk; [3] ARC and LRU-K (FAST'03, SIGMOD'93) both fail 0/5 caps, refuting the "parameter-free adaptive baseline already works" objection and showing no drop-in replacement; [4] Engineering cost to replace battle-tested LRU in SGLang/vLLM with frequency-aware variants for a <11% recompute delta that does not translate to end-to-end latency; [5] Corpus scale (72 CC sessions, 3290 reuse units) is not demonstrated to represent production agent traffic mix; [6] Slot-count capacity model is a harness artifact; a mass-based capacity would require rewriting the Belady oracle (harness-verbatim constraint).
RATIONALE: RE-B0 reproduces the premise: far_share=0.188 (EXP-0057 0.189) and Belady saves max 11.0% over LRU at frac 0.1, satisfying >=5%. FIX-2 killer baselines were genuinely run: ARC 0/5 caps >=0.50, LRU-K(2) 0/5 caps >=0.50, confirming both fail below the custom pin's 1/5. FIX-5 matched residency is honored: pinned blocks charged against C, and the lone pin "win" at frac 0.7 occurs on a 0.6%-of-LRU gap, so no capacity illusion. Kill is robust: HHI=0.08 (<0.20, no session domination), 0/15 static-pin cells survive BH or Bonferroni (pin2@0.7 boot-p=0.49). The ship-LRU guidance is actionable: SGLang-LFU is worse than LRU where it would matter, ARC/LRU-K do not capture the gap, and the oracle win is confined to tight-cache microbenchmark regimes. This reinforces DEAD-0019 from the policy side: the agent-KV Belady gap is oracle-only (requires near-oracle future-step knowledge per KVFlow 2507.07400), not reachable by cheap history-only policies. Prompt version: v001.
```
